A||N||5||cscougar||Replacing Front Suspension||||1||
Z||000000||cscougar||08-17-2000||08:29 PM||||I have a low mileage 85 SE that I want to use as a  donor for all the front suspension.  My 85 GT has about 200,000 miles on it and needs new ball joints and all.   How much trouble would it be to take the entire front suspension from the donor car and install it onto my GT?   The donor car was hit in the rear and totalled but the front end is undamaged.<BR> <BR>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Rodrv6||08-17-2000||08:54 PM||||It shouldn't be a huge job.  It is possible to unbolt the entire crossmember and remove the front suspension as a unit. Unless the crossmember bolts are rusted, then it is a little more fun.  You can also dissassemble it fairly easily.  Separating the ball joints from the spindle is probably the hardest thing to do.  Also, watch out for the spring--it is under load and must be removed carefully or it can fly out and do some damage!<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE||||reg||1
Z||000002||cscougar||08-17-2000||09:37 PM||||I would like to remove the entire front suspension, including spindles from the donor car and install it on the GT.  Would you know where the bolts are located that would allow me to take the suspension down as a unit?<P>Ted||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-18-2000||01:53 PM||||The front suspension is not a single unit on the cross member from what I can remember. The lower arms and rack are on the cross and the uppers go to the car frame. The sway bar goes to the frame too I think. been a couple months since I been under there.<P>That being the case you'd be faster moving the control arms and whatever else in parts. <P>either way you'd have to get the thing aligned once you are done so there's no cost saved there.||||reg||1
Z||000004||cscougar||08-18-2000||02:35 PM||||From looking at pictures of the suspension, it looks like I can unbolt all the pieces from the frame and crossmember and then lower the entire suspension as a unit.   I guess I will try it on the donor car this weekend and see what happens.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Rodrv6||08-18-2000||04:15 PM||||On the pre 88's, both upper and lower control arms are bolted to the crossmember.  The lower arms are also attatched to the chassis at the rear pivot point, but they can be detached there and removed with the crossmember.  The steering rack is also bolted to the crossmember, but the anti-roll bar is attatched to the chassis and will need to be removed separatly.  The upper end of the shocks attatch to the chassis.  The crossmember has five bolts on each side holding it to the chassis.  3 of them are horizontal and are located above the upper spring seat.  The other 2 are vertical and are under and behind the top of the coil spring.  There are also a couple of braces running forward from the crossmember to the chassis.  It's not a thirty minute job, but if you are wanting the entire suspension it is faster than doing 1 piece at a time.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE||||reg||10
